expand: keep arithmetic in int64 and stop brace sequences at their end

Arithm returned int, converting from the int64 that atoi parses, which
truncates on 32-bit platforms (the TODO(v4) in arith.go). Arithmetic is
now int64 throughout; slice offsets and lengths are clamped in int64
before indexing, and array subscripts that do not fit in an int are an
error instead of silently wrapping.

Brace sequences stepped with n += incr and compared against the end, so
an endpoint near the int64 limits, or a step of -9223372036854775808,
overflowed past the end and never terminated. Braces then allocated
without bound, and BracesSeq only stopped at its element limit:

	{9223372036854775806..9223372036854775807}
	{1..3..-9223372036854775808}

Sequences now walk unsigned offsets that stop at the end, and character
sequences step a byte, so no converted value can fall outside its type.

\x escapes are parsed as 8 bits and \u and \U escapes above
unicode.MaxRune give U+FFFD explicitly rather than through an unchecked
conversion; the output is unchanged.

